% ============================================================================
% past-tense-ed-sound-effect — for the /id/ past-tense -ed sound, the extra
% pronunciation EFFECT the same already-quoted 7ESL rule states, decoded
% from a span already sitting unused inside `past-tense-ed-sound.adj`'s own
% header (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `past-tense-ed-sound.adj`
% (`past_tense_ed_sound(word, sound)`, walked/lived/wanted →
% t_sound/d_sound/id_sound): that table's own header already quotes,
% verbatim, the 7ESL rule for the /id/ sound in full --
%
%     "Final -ed is pronounced /id/ after 'T', and 'D' sounds. The sound
%      /id/ adds a whole syllable to a word."
%
% -- but the table's `word, sound` schema only had room for the SOUND
% itself (id_sound), not the second sentence's pronunciation-EFFECT claim
% (that the /id/ sound adds a whole extra syllable to the word). New
% `past_tense_ed_sound_effect(sound, effect)` table decodes that second
% sentence as its own row:
%
%   id_sound → adds_a_whole_syllable
%     "The sound /id/ adds a whole syllable to a word."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? past_tense_ed_sound_effect(id_sound, $Effect)  % adds_a_whole_syllable
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `past_tense_ed_sound_effect(sound, effect)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the
% effect WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on any sound that is not the one tabled here (it never invents an
% effect). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind an effect and recall which
% sound carries it:
%
%     ? past_tense_ed_sound_effect($Sound, adds_a_whole_syllable)  % id_sound
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table: of the three -ed sounds the parent
% table names, only the /id/ rule's own sentence states a pronunciation
% EFFECT beyond the sound value itself -- the /t/ and /d/ rules each state
% only which voiced/voiceless sounds trigger them, with no comparable
% effect clause. A recall on either of those two real, already-tabled
% sounds -- `t_sound` (walked's rule: "Final -ed is pronounced /t/ after
% all voiceless sounds.", no effect stated) -- correctly abstains rather
% than inventing an effect for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME span already quoted
% inside `past-tense-ed-sound.adj`'s own header (its worked-examples
% table, "rule (verbatim)" column, wanted/id_sound row), from the SAME
% 7ESL "Pronunciation of ED" article that table already cites -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of that
% already-verified quote. `trust consensus` -- the same tier the parent
% table already carries (a general ESL-learning site, not a primary
% government or research-institution source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table past_tense_ed_sound_effect {
    columns sound, effect

    row (id_sound, adds_a_whole_syllable)

    source "The sound /id/ adds a whole syllable to a word."
    locator "https://7esl.com/pronunciation-of-ed/"
    trust consensus
}
